sections.s2_technical:
You declare it like a ThreadLocal: `InheritableThreadLocal<String> correlationId = new InheritableThreadLocal<>()`. When a thread sets a value and then creates a new thread, the new thread's map is seeded with the parent's value automatically.

```java
InheritableThreadLocal<String> ctx = new InheritableThreadLocal<>();
ctx.set("REQ-123");
new Thread(() -> System.out.println(ctx.get())).start();  // prints REQ-123 (inherited)
```

The inheritance happens inside the `Thread` constructor: it copies the parent's inheritable-thread-local map into the child. You can control the copy by overriding `childValue(T parentValue)`, which is useful when the value is mutable and you want the child to get a defensive copy rather than share the same object.

```java
InheritableThreadLocal<List<String>> tags = new InheritableThreadLocal<>() {
    @Override protected List<String> childValue(List<String> parentValue) {
        return new ArrayList<>(parentValue);  // child gets its own copy, not a shared list
    }
};
```

The critical trap is the timing: inheritance is a one-time copy at thread creation, not at task submission. In a `ThreadPoolExecutor`, worker threads are created lazily but only once, by the thread that triggers their creation — effectively the pool's creator or the first submitter — and then those same workers are reused for thousands of unrelated tasks. So a worker captures the inheritable value that existed when it was born and keeps returning that stale value for every later task, regardless of who submitted the task.

```java
InheritableThreadLocal<String> user = new InheritableThreadLocal<>();
user.set("admin");                                   // whoever built the pool
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);  // workers inherit "admin"
// Later, request thread sets user to "rajesh" and submits a task:
user.set("rajesh");
pool.submit(() -> System.out.println(user.get()));   // prints "admin" (stale!), NOT "rajesh"
```

This produces two failure modes. First, staleness: the task sees the creator's value, not the submitter's, so request-scoped data like a correlation id, tenant id, or authenticated user is simply wrong. Second, leakage: if any code sets the thread-local on a worker and does not clear it, the next task on that reused worker inherits leftover state, so one customer's security context can bleed into another's request — a serious vulnerability in a bank.

The correct approach for pools is to propagate context explicitly per task. You capture the value on the submitting thread, pass it into the task (for example by wrapping the Runnable so it sets the thread-local at the start and clears it in a finally block), and never rely on automatic inheritance. Modern frameworks do exactly this with task decorators, and newer libraries like the micrometer context-propagation library or scoped values in recent Java provide structured ways to carry context across pool boundaries safely.

```java
String current = user.get();                         // capture on submitting thread
pool.submit(() -> {
    user.set(current);                               // set explicitly on the worker
    try { doWork(); } finally { user.remove(); }     // ALWAYS clear to prevent leakage
});
```

A senior nuance: InheritableThreadLocal is genuinely useful only for threads you create directly and that live for one logical flow, such as a dedicated worker you spawn per request. For any pooled or reused executor, treat automatic inheritance as a bug waiting to happen and propagate context explicitly with a wrapper and a finally-clear.

